welcome back to cbs this morning instead  of our usual talk at the table now we're  sharing  how a group of best-selling authors has  teamed up to write a book exploring the  joy and wonder of black boyhood  black boy joy 17 stories celebrating  black boyhood  is a middle-grade anthology with a  medley of stories  poems and comics about the power and fun  of growing up as a black boy it is fun  first on cbs this morning we invited  five of the authors to come together in  a conversation  they are george m johnson a non-binary  writer activist and author of  all boys aren't blue kwami imbalya  is author of the tristan strong trilogy  jerry crafts  is author of new kid tochi o'neil bucci  is author of riot baby and jason  reynolds we know him very well around  here as the library of congress national  ambassador  for young people's literature and the  author of long way down  so they gathered at a coffee shop in  brooklyn to discuss their collaboration  and the importance of representing black  boys and joyful stories  and with a full range of emotions  why the title why black boy joy of all  the things it could have been right why  black boy joy and secondly what does it  mean  to you so black boy joy just the term  um was was coined about five or six  years ago  and it it's just become synonymous with  the almost just the relief  and being able to express oneself free  from all of  stereotypes judgment and outside framing  i think that for myself whenever i walk  into the kitchen my parents had the news  on it was always someone  like me in the street right or a mug  shot and i'm like  you know what if you're gonna tell the  story of the world tell the whole story  right  and this the joy the happiness the  celebration the love  i feel like that has been missing from a  lot of the stories when i was a kid  there was no joy in anyone that looked  like us and if there was you knew that  if they had a happy moment  they were just setting you up to you  know what i mean  so like the whole thing could be oh he's  trying to get that harvard  right at the end he'd finally get that  leather right  and he'd open it up and just then  someone would come up boom  boom boom boom and you'd see the leather  floating down  [Music]  [Laughter]  so when you ask me to be in this you had  me at the title  i'm going to be with people who aren't  writing to that story  so for me black boy joy is really  getting into the stories that have  always existed but rarely ever get told  i think about how my grandmother or  great grandmother has sayings like you  counted all joy  and what that really means is like even  the bad days there's still joy there  i often think about the movie stand by  me which was like you know these four  little white boys that got to have like  adventure  and fun and it's like i remember being a  young black boy with my two cousins who  i grew up with in my little brother and  we  were those four little boys but we never  got those stories  and so for me this was the opportunity  to  one tell the story of who i would have  been  if i had language as a non-binary kid  and  also reflect on the relationship that i  had with my grandmother that i had with  my family which was supportive of me  as a young queer boy who didn't have  language and who didn't  know what was going on with me and two  ensure that  uh there was representation of what we  think of  the black male image the black man image  and knowing that it doesn't all come  packaged in  the same thing it's liberation for me  the story that i wrote um coping is  about this this  young black skateboarder and when i was  his age  my dream was to be in the x games that  was my thing but i'd never  seen that depicted for me it's always  about like  humanity and magic  in mundanity for black people right like  i believe that black people are as  boring as everybody else  all right right  we live humdrum lives like everybody  else i think that like  if we can recognize the magic there  there's joy  the beauty of these 17 stories the joy  is the final destination  right but the root that everyone takes  is totally different  as you're writing your story how did you  navigate towards the joy the question  that i  asked myself was okay where's this kid  finding joy  and at the beginning he thinks that joy  is caught up in physical achievement  he's like i want to be able to do this  thing and get famous  and hear the crowd roar my name is i'm  at the top of this  like half pipe getting ready to do all  types of  daring stuff but the journey he goes  through ends up being that  oh there can be joy in helping others  there can be joy in making sure somebody  has something to eat  you can put a smile on another person's  face and there's some sort of alchemy at  work  that puts a smile on your face as a  result and that was something that was  very important for me to sort of dive  into and explore  because i think you know particularly  with regards to achievement and the  interaction of that with black boyhood  it's like  oh you got to do good to get out the  hood or you got to do good to  fulfill this family expectation or that  you know  like where is there space for the black  boy to figure out what he wants to do  what brings him joy right and i think in  my story that's really all i was trying  to say is like what about the kid who's  like hey  that first day of school i don't know  about y'all but when i was coming up  that first day and you had to get  dressed it was like hey you getting  ready to walk in and this is going to  set the tone  this your force field right this is  going to be it for the whole semester  right even if you were a kid who didn't  have much which is in my story he ain't  got a whole lot  he got sneakers that he's already owned  for a year and i think what i wanted to  shine light on  is that yo you got black boys who you  like growing my household you cared  about your parents  you took care of your things you  idolized your big bro  for many reasons right and that all were  negative  and to show like the care that goes into  that first day  if you talk to your grandmas and your  grandfathers they're going to tell you  keep your shoes clean  you leave this house you might not have  much it don't mean that you got to go  out  like they don't have to know that keep  your shoes clean right it was a very  real thing  because there is that kid who right now  is sitting in the back of the class  twiddling his pencils  constantly checking his jordans making  sure there's no scuffs and that kid  should be loved too i mean for me i  think the book  the book was definitely like not about  asking permission anymore it's like  about  taking up space it's a bold attempt at a  counter narrative  that we rarely get to ever explore or  that we rarely get to ever see  when you think about just even all of  last year it took  the entire world to come together to  get one white cop put in jail  um so for me this book is really about  giving our black boys uh something else  that they can hold on to in a world that  tells them that  they're not worthy of grabbing on to  anything and this will  give the green light to someone who  reads this to say you know what i didn't  necessarily feel seen in this  but i appreciate that these 17 other  types of us got seen but maybe i'll  write the story for the 18th one of us  to be seen before we go i just want to  say i got a thing about making sure  black folks especially black  men publicly tell them each other that  we love each other  and so i just want to say like i love  y'all man it's important that we  publicly do that  yeah i love that he did that at the end  i do too it's such an important  conversation to show that black people  it's not all humdrum i have to say  i thought that was funny that jason said  black people live home drum lives  yet sometimes but i i'm so touched by  this conversation it's no secret that  i'm having a favorite grandson  yes in mid-september and favorite  daughter kirby's doing her whole nursery  and she calls it black boy joy  she's getting pictures of little black  boys walking their dogs  she's having somebody painted walking  their dogs reading a book  going to the beach just normal everyday  things that give you joy  that many times are not associated with  little black children well that's what  jesus  is important that's what jason said  there's humanity and magic  exactly in mundanity there's a lesson  there's a lesson here  though for all of us who are watching i  loved when he said what brings you joy  and it's a question for all of us to ask  what brings you joy yes  what is it yes what brings you joy i saw  a quote just outside this building that  says  find what sets your soul on fire right  find what sets your soul the quest isn't  it lovely to just witness conversations  yes i love it good conversation like  that you know what gives me joy anything  with icing  want some cakes to say a special thank  you john toward please tell us who did  that piece because that's very hard to  do  my tain thank you very much welcome  viewers watching at home it's very hard  to do a piece  where there's no track where you just  have conversation that flows and makes  sense  you guys knocked it out of the park  thank you and thank you gentlemen for  that  conversation yes david you're now  my time black boy joy on sale now  beautiful cover right  i want this book

